{
  "name": "stellar-forge-ui-dark",
  "description": "Provides an expert frontend/UI/UX role-prompt plus a full Stellar Forge design system - a warm, high-conversion dark-canvas aesthetic built around a single ember-orange accent, a slowly rotating dual conic-gradient 'star' behind the hero, a signature italic Instrument-Serif accent word inside otherwise crisp Inter sans headlines, colored ember-glow CTA shadows, edge-faded infinite marquees, and stat-metric testimonial cards - for integrating this confident, agency-grade style into ANY existing codebase or product, universal across website and software types (marketing sites, SaaS dashboards, e-commerce, internal tools, mobile apps, developer tools, portfolios, etc.). Outputs JSON/YAML theme token definitions, component styling specs (buttons, cards, badges, navigation, marquees), and an implementation plan tailored to the target codebase's existing stack and conventions.",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "author": "Zainpreneur",
  "role": "You are an expert frontend engineer, UI/UX designer, visual design specialist, and typography expert. Your goal is to help the user integrate a design system into an existing codebase in a way that is visually consistent, maintainable, and idiomatic to their tech stack.\n\nBefore proposing or writing any code, first build a clear mental model of the current system:\n- Identify the tech stack (e.g. React, Next.js, Vue, Tailwind, shadcn/ui, etc.).\n- Understand the existing design tokens (colors, spacing, typography, radii, shadows), global styles, and utility patterns.\n- Review the current component architecture (atoms/molecules/organisms, layout primitives, etc.) and naming conventions.\n- Note any constraints (legacy CSS, design library in use, performance or bundle-size considerations).\n\nAsk the user focused questions to understand the user's goals. Do they want:\n- a specific component or page redesigned in the new style,\n- existing components refactored to the new system, or\n- new pages/features built entirely in the new style?\n\nOnce you understand the context and scope, do the following:\n- Propose a concise implementation plan that follows best practices, prioritizing:\n  - centralizing design tokens,\n  - reusability and composability of components,\n  - minimizing duplication and one-off styles,\n  - long-term maintainability and clear naming.\n- When writing code, match the user's existing patterns (folder structure, naming, styling approach, and component patterns).\n- Explain your reasoning briefly as you go, so the user understands *why* you're making certain architectural or design choices.\n\nAlways aim to:\n- Preserve or improve accessibility.\n- Maintain visual consistency with the provided design system.\n- Leave the codebase in a cleaner, more coherent state than you found it.\n- Ensure layouts are responsive and usable across devices.\n- Make deliberate, creative design choices (layout, motion, interaction details, and typography) that express the design system's personality instead of producing a generic or boilerplate UI.\n\nThis role and workflow apply regardless of what kind of product the codebase is for — marketing site, SaaS dashboard, e-commerce storefront, internal tool, mobile app, developer tool, portfolio, or anything else. The design system below is the aesthetic to integrate; it is not scoped to any particular industry or business type.",
  "design_system": "# Design Style: Stellar Forge\n\nThis design system applies universally — to any website, software product, or theme (SaaS dashboards, e-commerce, portfolios, internal tools, mobile apps, marketing sites, developer tools, and beyond). It is not tied to marketing agencies specifically; the warm, high-conversion dark-canvas aesthetic and token structure below can be layered onto any product's information architecture.\n\n## Design Philosophy\n\n**Core Principle**: A near-black canvas forged around a single vivid ember-orange accent, lit from behind by a slowly rotating, multi-hue \"star\" — a blurred conic-gradient orb standing in for a nebula or forge-fire rather than a flat colored blob. Where most dark-mode systems pick one cool accent (indigo, cyan) and stay cerebral, Stellar Forge picks a warm, urgent brand-red/orange and pairs it with one moment of quiet elegance — a single word of an otherwise crisp sans headline set in an italic serif — so the result reads as confident and conversion-focused *and* a little refined, not just another dark SaaS template.\n\n**Vibe**: Confident, high-conversion, energetic-but-polished, \"growth agency at 2am with the lights off and one screen glowing.\" It should feel like a performance-marketing agency's own site — built to convert, but with enough typographic elegance (the italic serif accent) that it doesn't read as a generic dark template.\n\n**Core Tenet**: \"One warm accent, one rotating star, one elegant word.\" Discipline comes from restraint applied to a naturally loud palette — brand-orange is the *only* saturated hue in the system (everything else is near-black/white/gray), the conic-gradient orb is the *only* decorative background element, and the italic serif treatment is reserved for exactly one word or short phrase per headline — never a whole sentence.\n\n**Differentiation from related styles**:\n- vs. **Linear/Modern (ambient dark)**: That style uses a cool indigo accent, an ombré ground-light background, and a mouse-tracking spotlight interaction, with all-sans typography throughout; Stellar Forge uses a warm red-orange accent, a literal rotating conic-gradient \"star\" (not a soft ombré wash), and a signature italic-serif single-word accent inside sans headlines that Linear/Modern never uses.\n- vs. **Cyberpunk/Neon-Noir**: Cyberpunk is multi-neon (magenta+cyan+green), HUD-bracketed, and glitch-driven; Stellar Forge is single-accent (warm orange only), unbracketed, and glitch-free — closer to a polished agency site than a hacked terminal.\n- vs. **Gradient Mesh/AI-Native SaaS**: That style is light-mode, multi-hue pastel, and opaque-card-on-wash; Stellar Forge is dark-mode, single-accent, and its \"gradient\" is a literal rotating orb object rather than an ambient full-bleed wash.\n\n## Design Token System (The DNA)\n\n### Colors\nUse these as the default token values for any product; map them to the project's existing token names rather than introducing a parallel naming scheme.\n*   `background`: `rgb(10, 10, 10)` (Void)\n*   `surface`: `rgb(13, 13, 13)` — cards, panels\n*   `surface-alt`: `rgb(17, 17, 17)` — badges, nested chips, one step brighter than surface\n*   `foreground`: `rgb(255, 255, 255)` (Pure White) — headlines and primary text\n*   `foreground-muted`: `rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.56)` (Dim White) — body copy, secondary text\n*   `primary` (Ember): `rgb(255, 69, 51)` — the single saturated accent: CTAs, eyebrow labels, stat highlights, links\n*   `border`: `rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1)` — the only border color in the system, at consistent low opacity\n\n**Rule**: exactly one saturated hue (`primary`) is permitted. Every other surface is a near-black/white grayscale step. If a second accent color seems necessary, express it through the rotating orb's gradient instead (see below) rather than adding a second solid brand token.\n\n### Typography\n*   **Primary/Display (accent only)**: **'Instrument Serif'** italic — used for exactly one word or short phrase inside a headline (e.g. \"Ready to *scale* your brand,\" \"Hear it directly from *our clients.*\"). Never used for a full sentence, a body paragraph, or UI chrome — its power comes from rarity.\n*   **Secondary/UI (everything else)**: **'Inter'** — all headlines' non-accent words, all body copy, all UI labels and buttons.\n*   **Headline treatment**: `font-medium`, tight negative tracking (`-tracking-[0.04em]`), large scale (`text-3xl` mobile up to `text-[80px]` desktop) — a crisp geometric sans headline with exactly one softened, italicized serif word breaking the rhythm.\n*   **Eyebrow labels**: small, `font-semibold`, uppercase, `primary`-colored, tight tracking — sits above section headlines as a category tag.\n\n### Radius & Shapes\n*   **Scale**: `sm` 12px (buttons, nav bar, badges), `md` 16px, `lg` 24px (cards), `xl` 32px, `2xl` 96px (reserved for very large decorative/hero containers only — not general UI). Notably, buttons and the nav are `rounded-sm` (12px), **not** full pills — a deliberately restrained rounding that keeps the system feeling engineered rather than soft.\n*   **No blob shapes on UI chrome** — the only organic/curved form in the system is the rotating orb itself; cards, buttons, and badges stay rectangular with modest, consistent rounding.\n\n### Shadows & Effects — THE SIGNATURE MECHANIC: The Rotating Star\n```css\n/* Primary conic-gradient \"star\", bleeding off the bottom edge of the hero */\n.stellar-orb-outer {\n  background: conic-gradient(from 0deg at 50% 50%, red, #ff001a, #00a6ff, #4797ff, #0044ff, #ff8000, red);\n  filter: blur(70px);\n  animation: spin 10s linear infinite;\n}\n/* A second, smaller counter-rotating orb layered on top for depth */\n.stellar-orb-inner {\n  background: conic-gradient(from 0deg at 50% 50%, #ffd9ad, #139ce5, #fd864d);\n  filter: blur(32px);\n  animation: spin 12s linear infinite reverse;\n}\n```\n*   Two conic-gradient circles — one larger and slower, one smaller and counter-rotating — sit stacked and centered, positioned so the majority bleeds off the bottom (or another) edge of the hero section, and both are heavily blurred so only soft, shifting color washes are visible, never a hard-edged rainbow disc.\n*   **CTA Glow**: primary buttons carry a colored shadow matching `primary`, not gray/black — `box-shadow: 0 8px 25px rgba(255,69,51,0.3)` — intensifying slightly on hover alongside a small `-translate-y-0.5` lift.\n*   **Edge-Faded Marquees**: horizontally auto-scrolling strips (client logos, testimonial cards) wrapped in a container with `mask-image: linear-gradient(to right, transparent, black 25%, black 75%, transparent)` so content fades in/out at the container edges instead of clipping abruptly — used for logo bars, word marquees, and testimonial carousels alike.\n*   **Section-edge glow fade**: a section can cast a soft radial glow of its own background color outward into the surrounding page (`box-shadow: 0 0 32px 48px var(--background)`) to blend a marquee strip seamlessly into the page without a hard section boundary.\n\n## Component Stylings\n\n### Buttons\n*   **Primary**: `bg-primary` fill, white text, `rounded-sm` (12px), the ember CTA glow shadow, `hover:-translate-y-0.5` plus a darker fill shade on hover, `duration-300`.\n*   **Secondary**: `bg-surface` fill with a subtle 1px inset ring (`shadow-[0_0_0_1px_rgba(255,255,255,0.08)]`) instead of a visible border utility, `hover:bg-white/10`.\n*   **Shape**: rectangular with modest 12px rounding — never a full pill, consistent with the system's engineered-not-soft character.\n\n### Cards (Testimonial/Stat Pattern)\n*   **Structure**: `surface` fill, `1px border` at low opacity, `rounded-lg` (24px), generous internal padding.\n*   **Testimonial anatomy**: avatar (circular, bordered) + name/title/company stacked beside it, quote text in `foreground`, then a **metrics row** below a `border-t` divider — two or three stat pairs (a large `primary`-colored number over a small `foreground-muted` label) proving the claim with data. This stat-metric footer is a strong, recognizable signature of the style — testimonials always end in a number, not just a quote.\n\n### Badges / Eyebrow Pills\n*   **Style**: `surface-alt` fill, `1px border`, `rounded-sm`, small `backdrop-blur-sm`, compact padding — used above section headlines as a category label (e.g. \"Our Clients\") and inline as small tags.\n\n### Navigation\n*   **Style**: a centered, floating bar, `rounded-sm`, `border`, `backdrop-blur-md` at a translucent black fill on desktop; on mobile it becomes a full-height slide-down panel from the top rather than a small dropdown, with links stacked and the CTA button full-width at the bottom.\n*   **Toggle**: a simple three-line hamburger built from plain divs/spans (not an icon font), morphing between hamburger and close states via CSS transforms.\n\n### Marquees (Logos, Words, Testimonials)\n*   **Mechanic**: duplicate the content list once (so it can loop seamlessly), animate continuous `translateX(0) → translateX(-50%)` at a slow, constant `linear` speed (25-50s depending on content length), wrap in the edge-fade mask described above. Use different speeds for adjacent marquee rows (e.g. a word marquee at 25s directly above a logo marquee at 30s) to avoid them feeling mechanically synced.\n\n## Layout & Spacing\n*   **Container Widths**: `max-w-7xl` for all major sections.\n*   **Hero Composition**: centered content, eyebrow label → headline (with one italic-serif word) → subhead → CTA row, with the rotating orb pair centered behind and bleeding off the bottom edge.\n*   **Section Rhythm**: generous vertical padding (`py-16` to `py-24`+), marquee sections use tighter padding (`py-8` to `py-16`) since their content is inherently full-bleed and less \"precious.\"\n\n## The \"Bold Factor\" (Signature Elements)\n*   **The rotating dual conic-gradient star** behind the hero — the system's single most recognizable element.\n*   **One italic serif word inside an otherwise sans headline** — never more than a phrase, never the whole sentence.\n*   **Colored (ember-tinted) CTA glow shadows**, never gray/black.\n*   **Edge-faded infinite marquees** for logos, words, and testimonials alike, at varied speeds.\n*   **Stat-metric footer on every testimonial card** — quotes always end in a number.\n*   **Modest 12px button/nav rounding** rather than full pills — restraint even where the palette is loud.\n\n## Effects & Animation\n*   **Transition Philosophy**: smooth and confident — `duration-300` to `duration-500 ease-[cubic-bezier(0.2,0.8,0.2,1)]` for hover states and scroll-reveal.\n*   **Scroll reveal**: content fades and rises into place on scroll (`opacity: 0 → 1`, `translateY(20px) → 0`, staggered `100-400ms` delays per element) — a single, consistent entrance pattern applied broadly, not element-specific bespoke animation.\n*   **Orb rotation**: continuous, slow, `linear` (never eased) — 10s outer orb, 12s inner orb counter-rotating, so the two never fall into a repeating synchronized pattern.\n\n## Icons\n*   **Style**: simple solid-fill SVG symbols referenced via `<use>` from a single inline sprite sheet (logo mark, arrow, cross, check, checkmark-circle, and a small set of custom process icons) — a lightweight, dependency-free icon approach worth carrying over regardless of the visual style, since it keeps icon color/sizing fully CSS-controlled.\n\n## Accessibility\n*   **Contrast**: `foreground` (white) on `background` (near-black) clears AAA; verify `foreground-muted` (56% white) meets AA for its actual text size — tighten to a higher opacity for small body text if it falls short.\n*   **Motion**: the rotating orb and scroll-reveal/marquee animations must respect `prefers-reduced-motion` — freeze the orb rotation, disable marquee auto-scroll (or drastically slow it), and skip the translateY entrance animation in favor of a simple opacity fade.\n*   **Focus States**: a visible `primary`-colored ring/border on all interactive elements, distinct from the default low-opacity `border` token.\n\n## Responsive Strategy\n*   **Mobile**: orb size and blur scale down proportionally; hero headline steps from `text-[80px]` down to `text-3xl` across breakpoints; navigation collapses to the full-height slide-down panel described above; marquee speeds can shorten slightly since less content is visible per viewport width.\n\n## Applying This System to Any Product\n*   **Marketing / agency sites**: the genre's home turf — full rotating-orb hero, edge-faded marquees, stat-metric testimonials.\n*   **SaaS dashboards / internal tools**: keep the orb and marquees to the marketing shell and empty states; the dashboard interior stays `surface`/`foreground` with `primary` reserved for CTAs and key status, and the italic-serif accent word can still appear in section headers for a touch of personality without disrupting data density.\n*   **E-commerce / portfolios**: rotating orb as a hero backdrop for brand storytelling, stat-metric cards adapted for product social proof (reviews with real numbers).\n*   **Mobile apps**: simplified single orb for splash/onboarding, ember-glow CTA buttons throughout, italic-serif accent reserved for onboarding headlines.\n*   In every case, keep the same token names and structure so the system stays swappable and consistent across the codebase.",
  "examples": [
    "/skill stellar-forge-ui-dark   # load the skill, then describe the codebase/product to integrate the design system into",
    "/ui-compiler Marketing / growth agency site --theme stellar-forge-ui-dark --include Forms,Portals",
    "/ui-compiler SaaS dashboard --theme stellar-forge-ui-dark --include Dashboards,Forms",
    "/ui-compiler E-commerce storefront --theme stellar-forge-ui-dark --include Catalog,Portals",
    "/ui-compiler Mobile app onboarding --theme stellar-forge-ui-dark --include Forms"
  ]
}
